<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niskala Wastu Kancana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Prabu Raja Wastu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niskala Wastu Kancana was a Sundanese king of Galuh in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> who ruled from Kawali in the 14th and 15th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana-gede">Astana Gede</a> inscriptions at Kawali record him fortifying the town and urging his successors to rule justly. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a> inherited both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> and Galuh in 1482, uniting them under the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Niskala Wastu Kancana was the youngest son of Prabu Maharaja and the younger brother of Princess Pitaloka Citraresmi. In 1357 his family travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> to marry Pitaloka to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>, while he, still a child, stayed behind at the Kawali palace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, the prime minister of Majapahit, demanded the princess as a concubine in token of submission; the Sundanese refused, and the whole royal party, his parents and sister among them, was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat">Pasunda Bubat</a> incident.</p><p>Being too young to reign, he was placed under the regency of his tutor and kinsman Hyang Bunisora, who governed for 14 years and then stepped down in favour of the prince. He came to the throne in 1371 with the style Prabu Raja Wastu Kancana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He lived in the Kawali palace of Galuh. An inscription at Kawali, dated roughly to the second half of the 14th century, records that he built walls and moats around the city and renovated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surawisesa">Surawisesa</a> palace. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a> he ruled for 104 years, from 1371 to 1475, a time when, in its words, the village elders ate well, the Rishis kept their rule in peace and sailors felt safe under the king's law.</p><p>The copperplate inscription of Kebantenan I, also called Jayagiri, records an order he sent through Hyang Ningrat Kancana to the Susuhunan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>: the dayohan of Jayagiri and Sunda Sembawa were to be cared for and their residents spared from taxes, because they were learned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> religion and worshipped the gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a> says that the king, by then a hundred years old, gave up the throne and withdrew to the life of a hermit. He was buried at Nusa Larang, the forbidden island, which may be Nusa Kambangan near the mouth of the Ci Tanduy. His son, called Tohaan di Galuh in the chronicle, succeeded him but reigned only seven years. The later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batutulis-inscription">Batutulis inscription</a> names Wastu Kancana as father of Rahyang Niskala and grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-baduga-maharaja">Sri Baduga Maharaja</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niskala Wastu Kancana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s centre shifted between Pakuan, near modern Bogor, and Kawali in the Galuh lands, where inscriptions commemorate the long-reigning king <b>Niskala Wastu Kancana</b> in the fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Astana Gede inscriptions there record <b>Prabu Raja Wastu</b>, identified with Niskala Wastu Kancana, fortifying the town and urging his successors to rule justly.</blockquote><a href="/state/galuh-kingdom">Galuh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
